1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,960
Every day in Hong Kong,
about four people die of liver cancer.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,821
That's 1,412 people in 2022 alone.

3
00:00:10,861 --> 00:00:17,302
Here, it's one of the three deadliest
cancers, alongside lung and colorectal.

4
00:00:17,342 --> 00:00:19,069
And it's not just us.

5
00:00:19,109 --> 00:00:24,960
Worldwide, it's the sixth most common
cancer, and the third deadliest.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,731
Here's the thing.
We already have medicines.

7
00:00:28,771 --> 00:00:30,960
Just not good enough ones.

8
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,707
The standard first treatment for advanced
liver cancer has already changed once,

9
00:00:37,747 --> 00:00:42,346
from Nexavar to a combo,
atezolizumab plus bevacizumab.

10
00:00:42,386 --> 00:00:47,996
But here, only about three in ten
patients are alive five years on.

11
00:00:48,036 --> 00:00:51,960
The study we follow calls
it a huge unmet need.

12
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,960
So... why not just make a better one?

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,960
Because that's way harder than it sounds.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,960
Like, ten-to-seventeen-years hard.

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,101
Start the search the year we were born,
and it might only be ready now.

16
00:01:06,141 --> 00:01:09,733
It can cost almost 2.8 billion US dollars.

17
00:01:09,773 --> 00:01:15,960
And of drugs tested in people,
only about one in ten reaches the market.

18
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,384
To see why,
let's follow one drug's journey.

19
00:01:19,424 --> 00:01:22,108
Every medicine walks the same road.

20
00:01:22,148 --> 00:01:26,855
Find a target. Find a hit,
a first molecule that works on it.

21
00:01:26,895 --> 00:01:30,201
Improve it.
Test it in cells, then animals.

22
00:01:30,241 --> 00:01:33,392
Then human trials: phase one, two, three.

23
00:01:33,432 --> 00:01:35,960
Then approval. Any step can fail.

24
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,286
Now here's where it gets interesting.

25
00:01:39,326 --> 00:01:40,286
In 2024,

26
00:01:40,326 --> 00:01:47,117
part of the Nobel Prize in Chemistry
went to Demis Hassabis and John Jumper,

27
00:01:47,157 --> 00:01:51,960
for AlphaFold,
an AI that predicts the shape of proteins.

28
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,765
So can AI walk the first steps faster?

29
00:01:54,805 --> 00:01:59,490
Let's follow one real case,
where AI tools took on liver cancer.

30
00:01:59,530 --> 00:02:02,960
And part of it starts right here,
in Hong Kong.

31
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:28,451
Hong Kong first. In 2011, a team at the
Chinese University of Hong Kong studied

32
00:02:28,491 --> 00:02:34,141
liver tumours from patients at
the Prince of Wales Hospital.

33
00:02:34,181 --> 00:02:39,831
One protein kept showing up raised:
CCRK, also called CDK20.

34
00:02:39,871 --> 00:02:47,132
Fast forward to 2023: an AI drug-discovery
study goes after the same protein,

35
00:02:47,172 --> 00:02:53,960
with co-authors at Insilico Medicine's
Hong Kong office in Science Park.

36
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,008
So our research question is: how can
computer-aided methods help scientists

37
00:03:00,048 --> 00:03:03,960
find and assess potential
cancer drug candidates?

38
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,960
And we'll answer it with one case study:
CDK20 in liver cancer.

39
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,960
We set ourselves two objectives.

40
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:20,322
One: to identify the major computer-aided
methods used in cancer drug discovery.

41
00:03:20,362 --> 00:03:23,911
Two: to evaluate how these methods help

42
00:03:23,951 --> 00:03:27,960
scientists assess
potential drug candidates.

43
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,433
So how did we check all this? We read.

44
00:03:31,473 --> 00:03:36,826
A lot. No lab work of our own:
this is a literature review.

45
00:03:36,866 --> 00:03:43,772
At the centre, one original study by
Ren and colleagues in Chemical Science,

46
00:03:43,812 --> 00:03:46,879
2023, with its supplementary data.

47
00:03:46,919 --> 00:03:50,444
Around it, the earlier Hong Kong study,

48
00:03:50,484 --> 00:03:54,283
papers behind two AI tools, a 2023 review,

49
00:03:54,323 --> 00:03:58,852
a textbook chapter,
and a 2026 expert Perspective,

50
00:03:58,892 --> 00:04:01,960
which we read only as an abstract.

51
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,580
For every source,
we asked: what kind is it,

52
00:04:05,620 --> 00:04:09,941
how much did we read,
and how strong is the evidence?

53
00:04:09,981 --> 00:04:14,055
Who did the work?
Were there controls and repeats?

54
00:04:14,095 --> 00:04:16,030
One thing worth knowing:

55
00:04:16,070 --> 00:04:20,802
most authors of the main study
work for Insilico Medicine,

56
00:04:20,842 --> 00:04:24,504
which built its AI tools.
They say so openly.

57
00:04:24,544 --> 00:04:27,960
Useful evidence, not an independent check.

58
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,284
So here's where we're going.
First, the lock.

59
00:04:31,324 --> 00:04:33,943
Then how AI helped cut a key for it.

60
00:04:33,983 --> 00:04:36,159
Then what happened in the lab.

61
00:04:36,199 --> 00:04:40,960
And finally, the big one:
how much did the computers really help?

62
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,960
Okay.
To get this, picture a lock and a key.

63
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,754
Inside your cells,
proteins do a lot of the work.

64
00:04:50,794 --> 00:04:55,399
Most drugs grab one protein
tightly and change what it does,

65
00:04:55,439 --> 00:04:58,960
slotting into a pocket
like a key into a lock.

66
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:04,719
Scientists call the lock the target,
and the key a candidate molecule.

67
00:05:04,759 --> 00:05:09,821
A good key fits its own lock tightly,
and opens no one else's.

68
00:05:09,861 --> 00:05:11,960
That's called selectivity.

69
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,531
Our lock is CDK20. It's an enzyme,
a protein with a chemical job.

70
00:05:17,571 --> 00:05:24,046
Its keyhole is a pocket where it normally
grabs a small molecule called ATP.

71
00:05:24,086 --> 00:05:29,960
Plug that pocket with the right key,
and the enzyme can't do its job.

72
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,775
Why this lock?
Remember the Hong Kong team.

73
00:05:33,815 --> 00:05:40,605
In 36 of 52 liver tumours, CDK20 was
higher than in nearby non-tumour tissue.

74
00:05:40,645 --> 00:05:46,194
High levels went with later-stage
tumours and shorter survival.

75
00:05:46,234 --> 00:05:51,960
And turning CDK20 down in liver
cancer cells slowed their growth.

76
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:53,775
So: a strong suspect.

77
00:05:53,815 --> 00:05:58,960
Not the only cause of liver cancer,
but a lock worth trying.

78
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:04,177
In 2023, an AI platform called
PandaOmics backed that hunch.

79
00:06:04,217 --> 00:06:07,395
It combed ten liver cancer data sets,

80
00:06:07,435 --> 00:06:12,612
over a thousand tumour samples
and hundreds of healthy ones,

81
00:06:12,652 --> 00:06:14,438
plus research papers.

82
00:06:14,478 --> 00:06:19,743
People checked its top 20 by hand,
and CDK20 came out on top,

83
00:06:19,783 --> 00:06:22,960
with no approved drug against it yet.

84
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:28,194
But here's the problem.
To design a key, you need to see the lock.

85
00:06:28,234 --> 00:06:31,208
And no one had measured CDK20's shape.

86
00:06:31,248 --> 00:06:33,905
That takes a lot of hard lab work,

87
00:06:33,945 --> 00:06:38,822
and only a small fraction of
proteins have ever been measured.

88
00:06:38,862 --> 00:06:40,329
Without a 3D shape,

89
00:06:40,369 --> 00:06:45,960
even computer predictions of binding
get expensive, slow and difficult.

90
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,090
So you've got a lock nobody can see.

91
00:06:49,130 --> 00:06:51,960
How do you cut a key in the dark?

92
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,775
So the team did something that
once sounded like science fiction.

93
00:07:21,815 --> 00:07:24,441
They asked an AI to guess the shape.

94
00:07:24,481 --> 00:07:29,997
Imagine a protein as a chain of building
blocks that folds into a 3D shape.

95
00:07:30,037 --> 00:07:32,960
The order of the blocks is the sequence.

96
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,330
AlphaFold learned from known
structures how sequences fold.

97
00:07:37,370 --> 00:07:42,960
Give it one, and it predicts the shape,
plus how sure it is about each part.

98
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,439
Before,
you'd need a lab-measured structure.

99
00:07:46,479 --> 00:07:47,439
Now?

100
00:07:47,479 --> 00:07:53,132
The team just downloaded a prediction,
for free, from a public database.

101
00:07:53,172 --> 00:07:54,951
According to the paper,

102
00:07:54,991 --> 00:07:59,960
this was the first time AlphaFold had
been used to find a hit like this.

103
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:01,186
But look closer.

104
00:08:01,226 --> 00:08:06,164
One end of the protein, a floppy tail,
had really low confidence.

105
00:08:06,204 --> 00:08:09,534
And in the model,
it lay across the keyhole,

106
00:08:09,574 --> 00:08:13,671
with one building block
sitting inside the ATP pocket.

107
00:08:13,711 --> 00:08:17,960
Trust it blindly,
and you'd cut keys for a blocked door.

108
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:19,960
So what did they do?

109
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,085
They cut the tail off.
The AI made the guess.

110
00:08:23,125 --> 00:08:24,960
A human caught the problem.

111
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,393
And this next part honestly blew my mind.

112
00:08:28,433 --> 00:08:32,495
Think about how chemists
have always found a key.

113
00:08:32,535 --> 00:08:35,593
Imagine a molecule. Make it. Test it.

114
00:08:35,633 --> 00:08:38,104
It fails. Tweak it. Try again.

115
00:08:38,144 --> 00:08:42,960
And every single round in a
real lab costs time and money.

116
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:48,290
Generative AI takes that loop
and moves it inside a computer.

117
00:08:48,330 --> 00:08:50,999
The platform here, Chemistry42,

118
00:08:51,039 --> 00:08:56,591
runs dozens of AI models side by side,
built on known chemistry.

119
00:08:56,631 --> 00:09:00,873
They invent new molecules
for this exact keyhole,

120
00:09:00,913 --> 00:09:05,067
score them, learn from the scores,
and go again.

121
00:09:05,107 --> 00:09:09,960
Its makers say it turns out
new structures within hours.

122
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,366
AI didn't replace the scientists.

123
00:09:12,406 --> 00:09:16,960
It ran their trial and error
inside a computer, at light speed.

124
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:20,646
The team gave it the
pocket's shape and one rule:

125
00:09:20,686 --> 00:09:23,956
every key must grip a
spot called the hinge.

126
00:09:23,996 --> 00:09:29,748
Its filters are built to toss designs
with reactive or possibly toxic groups,

127
00:09:29,788 --> 00:09:33,960
and to score how drug-like
and easy to make each one is.

128
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,581
It came back with 8,918 brand-new designs.

129
00:09:52,621 --> 00:09:58,012
Way too many to make.
So each key gets tried in a virtual lock.

130
00:09:58,052 --> 00:10:04,650
That's docking: the computer predicts
how a molecule might sit in the pocket,

131
00:10:04,690 --> 00:10:10,770
and scores the fit. Similar designs
get grouped, 54 make the shortlist,

132
00:10:10,810 --> 00:10:13,960
and chemists pick just seven to make.

133
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,960
Wait. So the computer picked the winners?

134
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,074
Not quite.
A docking score is a clue, not proof.

135
00:10:21,114 --> 00:10:22,960
Only the lab can tell.

136
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:30,627
So, the moment of truth.
Seven real molecules, made in a real lab.

137
00:10:30,667 --> 00:10:35,778
Do any actually grab CDK20?
Grip is measured as Kd: roughly,

138
00:10:35,818 --> 00:10:40,243
how much molecule it takes
to grab half the protein.

139
00:10:40,283 --> 00:10:41,960
Lower means tighter.

140
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:46,560
Six of them barely held on.
But one stood out.

141
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:47,960
Its name? 001.

142
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,216
001's Kd: 9.2 micromolar. The other six?

143
00:10:51,256 --> 00:10:55,448
All above 30. Much weaker.
And here's the wild part.

144
00:10:55,488 --> 00:10:57,727
The clock read just 30 days,

145
00:10:57,767 --> 00:11:01,960
from picking the target to
this first confirmed hit.

146
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,772
But then came a second test.

147
00:11:04,812 --> 00:11:10,094
Because grabbing a lock
isn't the same as jamming it.

148
00:11:10,134 --> 00:11:14,311
Does 001 actually stop CDK20 from working?

149
00:11:14,351 --> 00:11:22,345
That's measured as IC50: how much you
need to cut the enzyme's activity in half.

150
00:11:22,385 --> 00:11:25,458
For 001? Above 6,000 nanomolar.

151
00:11:25,498 --> 00:11:25,960
Weak.

152
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:29,960
The key went into the lock.
But it barely turned.

153
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,960
So was that it? Back to square one?

154
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:33,960
Not at all.

155
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:39,832
This is where the loop kicks in again,
now with real lab data inside.

156
00:11:39,872 --> 00:11:46,386
The results went back in, with a predicted
pose of how 001 sat in the pocket.

157
00:11:46,426 --> 00:11:49,875
Sixteen new designs. Six made and tested.

158
00:11:49,915 --> 00:11:52,960
All within 30 days of that first hit.

159
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:59,230
And one of them, 048,
grabbed about fifteen times tighter.

160
00:11:59,270 --> 00:12:06,690
Then the enzyme test. Remember,
001 needed more than 6,000 nanomolar.

161
00:12:06,730 --> 00:12:08,960
048 needed just 33.4.

162
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:09,960
This time, the key turned.

163
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,389
Then, the big step: living cells.

164
00:12:21,429 --> 00:12:28,661
After three days with 048, Huh7 liver
cancer cells, packed with CDK20,

165
00:12:28,701 --> 00:12:34,687
lost half their living-cell
signal at about 210 nanomolar.

166
00:12:34,727 --> 00:12:39,363
Comparison cells, HEK293,
needed about 1,700.

167
00:12:39,403 --> 00:12:41,960
Roughly eight times more.

168
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:48,753
So, the big question.
How much did the computers actually help?

169
00:12:48,793 --> 00:12:50,960
Let's line up before and now.

170
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:56,759
Seeing the lock. Before: hard lab
experiments, and without a 3D shape,

171
00:12:56,799 --> 00:13:00,155
design was expensive, slow and difficult.

172
00:13:00,195 --> 00:13:04,960
Now: a free prediction,
downloaded from a public database.

173
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:11,457
Finding a hit. Before:
screening real compounds by the millions,

174
00:13:11,497 --> 00:13:17,447
an approach a 2023 review says
has hit a costly bottleneck.

175
00:13:17,487 --> 00:13:24,960
Now: 8,918 designs inside a computer,
and just 13 molecules actually made.

176
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:26,926
Millions of compounds screened before.

177
00:13:26,966 --> 00:13:27,960
Here? Thirteen made.

178
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,960
Thirteen. That's honestly wild.

179
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:34,614
Time.
Before: a ten-to-seventeen-year road,

180
00:13:34,654 --> 00:13:38,352
with finding a hit just
the opening stretch.

181
00:13:38,392 --> 00:13:43,960
Now: 30 days to the first hit,
and a better key within another 30.

182
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:49,452
Improving the key. Before: every
change meant another costly lab round.

183
00:13:49,492 --> 00:13:54,479
Now: a predicted picture of the
first key guided sixteen designs,

184
00:13:54,519 --> 00:13:57,960
and the grip got about
fifteen times tighter.

185
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,868
To be fair,
these pairs come from different studies:

186
00:14:01,908 --> 00:14:06,376
they compare scale and speed,
not one race run side by side.

187
00:14:06,416 --> 00:14:10,960
But the paper itself calls the
work cost- and time-efficient.

188
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:15,250
Now imagine this keeps
working further down the road.

189
00:14:15,290 --> 00:14:21,484
Fewer failed molecules, lower costs,
fewer animal tests, less chemical waste.

190
00:14:21,524 --> 00:14:24,074
Computing costs electricity too.

191
00:14:24,114 --> 00:14:27,960
And the real prize?
New treatments for patients.

192
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,982
So where's 048 on the road? Target: found.

193
00:14:31,022 --> 00:14:33,428
Hit: found. Improving it: started.

194
00:14:33,468 --> 00:14:37,960
Cells: first tests, done.
Animals and clinical trials: not yet.

195
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:42,381
And here's what the end of
that road can look like.

196
00:14:42,421 --> 00:14:47,149
Some AI-assisted cancer drugs
have walked much further.

197
00:14:47,189 --> 00:14:51,223
In September 2026,
one of them, lirafugratinib,

198
00:14:51,263 --> 00:14:55,211
was approved in the US
for a bile duct cancer.

199
00:14:55,251 --> 00:14:58,418
Others failed along the way. And 048?

200
00:14:58,458 --> 00:15:02,059
Its makers chose not to take it to trials,

201
00:15:02,099 --> 00:15:05,960
and shared it with other
researchers instead.

202
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,870
048 is at the very start of the road.

203
00:15:08,910 --> 00:15:12,960
But for this case,
that start took weeks, not years.

204
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,960
So, what should happen next?
We'd suggest three things.

205
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:22,895
One: walk 048 further.
It's public now, so anyone could try.

206
00:15:22,935 --> 00:15:27,666
Test it in more liver cancer
cells and normal liver cells,

207
00:15:27,706 --> 00:15:33,423
then animals, and check how the body
handles it and what else it hits.

208
00:15:33,463 --> 00:15:36,960
The authors had already
listed these steps.

209
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:42,434
Two: check the AlphaFold model
against a lab-measured structure,

210
00:15:42,474 --> 00:15:43,802
once one exists.

211
00:15:43,842 --> 00:15:49,960
And feed every result, including the
failures, back into the next round.

212
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:54,901
Three: race them. An AI route
and a classic route, same target,

213
00:15:54,941 --> 00:15:57,882
same budget, same definition of a hit.

214
00:15:57,922 --> 00:16:01,960
Then we'd know exactly how
much faster AI really is.

215
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:05,960
So, back to where we started.

216
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,578
How can computer-aided
methods help scientists

217
00:16:09,618 --> 00:16:12,645
find and assess cancer drug candidates?

218
00:16:12,685 --> 00:16:17,129
In this case, at four points.
Picking a target from data.

219
00:16:17,169 --> 00:16:20,904
Predicting the lock's shape.
Designing new keys.

220
00:16:20,944 --> 00:16:23,342
And ranking which ones to make.

221
00:16:23,382 --> 00:16:26,960
That's our first objective,
met for this case.

222
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:32,268
And our second? These methods helped
the team assess candidates fast.

223
00:16:32,308 --> 00:16:36,652
Screening by the millions
became thirteen molecules made.

224
00:16:36,692 --> 00:16:39,114
And on a road measured in years,

225
00:16:39,154 --> 00:16:43,960
the first hit came in 30 days,
and round two made the key turn.

226
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,804
This works in cells, not yet in people.

227
00:16:46,844 --> 00:16:49,137
The computer narrows the search.

228
00:16:49,177 --> 00:16:50,960
Experiments still decide.

229
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:55,960
So next time you see a headline
like 'AI discovers a cancer drug'...

230
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:59,089
...ask where it really is on the road:

231
00:16:59,129 --> 00:17:02,960
computer, protein, cells,
animals, or patients?

232
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,341
AI didn't replace the scientists.

233
00:17:05,381 --> 00:17:08,517
It gave them trial and
